A single opening does not give a featureless amplitude. A convenient local form is $\psi_1(y)=\rho(y)e^{i\phi(y)},$ where $\rho(y)$ changes slowly while the phase may change rapidly. Its detection density is $\left|\psi_1(y)\right|^2=\rho(y)^2.$ The rapid phase cancels against its complex conjugate, leaving a smooth envelope. With a second displaced opening, $\psi_2(y)=\rho_2(y)e^{i\phi_2(y)},$ and the probability becomes $\left|\psi_1+\psi_2\right|^{2}
  =\rho_1^2+\rho_2^2
   +2\rho_1\rho_2\cos(\phi_1-\phi_2).$ The smooth one-aperture envelopes are modulated by the relative-phase fringes.

